@model List<QlWeb2.Areas.Admin.Controllers.CollectionController.Summary>
@{ ViewData["Title"] = "Content"; }

@*
    Three groups, because there are two steps and the screen should say so: you write into a
    library, then you choose from it on the home page. A flat grid of thirteen tiles hid that -
    "Home: Products" sat beside "Products" looking like a second, rival list of products.

    The count comes from the model. "Ten kinds of thing" was typed here when there were ten, and
    it was still saying ten with thirteen tiles underneath it.
*@
@{
    var shelves = Model.Where(s => s.Kind.PickFrom is not null).ToList();
    var maps = Model.Where(s => !s.Kind.CanAdd).ToList();
    var libraries = Model.Except(shelves).Except(maps).ToList();
}

<h1>Content</h1>
<p class="ad-sub">
    @Model.Count kinds of thing on the site. Open one to add, remove, reorder or hide an item;
    to change what an item says, open its page in the editor &mdash; you can see it there.
</p>

<h2 class="ad-group-title">Libraries</h2>
<p class="ad-sub">Where everything is written. Each item here has a page of its own, or a place on the home page.</p>
<partial name="_Kinds" model="libraries" />

<h2 class="ad-group-title">The home page</h2>
<p class="ad-sub">
    Which of the above the home page shows, and in what order. Nothing is written here &mdash;
    each card points at one item in a library, and takes its words, its picture and its link
    from there.
</p>
<partial name="_Kinds" model="shelves" />

<h2 class="ad-group-title">The two maps</h2>
<p class="ad-sub">
    The globe and the factory map. Items can be hidden, reordered and removed; adding one needs
    map coordinates, which are not words on a page.
</p>
<partial name="_Kinds" model="maps" />
